{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good evening, Mr. ___ . I’m Dr. ___ , one of the neurology team. I understand you came in because you were having trouble reading words?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:23:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. Last night, I was looking at a menu and couldn’t recognize simple words like 'burger' or 'fries.' I could see the letters, but the word just wouldn’t come to mind."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:24:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And did you have any other symptoms? Like weakness, slurred speech, or trouble understanding what people were saying?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I could talk fine and understand everything. It was just reading. After a few minutes, the word would finally click. But it kept happening today, so my doctor sent me here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:26:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s very important. This could be a sign of a transient ischemic attack, or even a small stroke. We’re going to run some tests to find out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:27:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A stroke? But I feel okay now. I even read the sign on the way in without trouble."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:28:35",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Sometimes symptoms like this come and go. But the fact that it happened at all means we need to take it seriously. You’ve had atrial fibrillation and a stroke before, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:29:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, about a year ago. And I was on Coumadin, but then they switched me to Pradaxa last month."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:30:25",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And are you still on Pradaxa?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I stopped it. It gave me stomach burning. I was off blood thinners for a few days, then restarted Coumadin about a week ago."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:32:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a key detail. Being off anticoagulation, even briefly, increases your stroke risk, especially with your history. We’ll check your INR to see if your Coumadin is working yet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:33:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I didn’t realize that could be so dangerous. I just thought I’d switch back safely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2183-03-30 02:34:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s understandable. But with atrial fibrillation, your heart can form clots even when you feel fine. We’ll make sure you’re protected going forward."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2183-03-30 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. We got your CT scan from last night. It shows a small area of concern in the left side of your brain, in the temporal lobe. That’s likely why you had trouble reading."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2183-03-30 08:16:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it was a stroke? But I’m not weak or slurring words."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2183-03-30 08:17:35",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, a small ischemic stroke. The part of your brain affected handles visual word recognition. That’s why your speech and movement were fine, but reading was hard."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2183-03-30 08:18:50",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I get better?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2183-03-30 08:19:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Most patients improve. Your symptoms are already better, which is a good sign. We’ll do an MRI to get more detail and confirm the cause."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2183-03-30 20:59:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We have the MRI results. It confirms a small stroke in the left posterior cerebral artery area. No major blockages in your brain arteries. The good news is no new damage since the CT."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2183-03-30 21:00:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what caused it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2183-03-30 21:01:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Most likely, a small clot from your heart traveled to your brain. That’s common with atrial fibrillation, especially when you’re not on proper anticoagulation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2183-03-30 21:02:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Because I stopped Pradaxa?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2183-03-30 21:03:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Even a few days off increases risk. Your INR was just checked—it’s now therapeutic, so the Coumadin is working. But we need to prevent this from happening again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2183-03-30 21:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll take it every day. No more stopping."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2183-03-30 21:05:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. We’ll keep you on warfarin for now. Your primary care and anticoagulation clinic will follow your INR closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2183-03-31 06:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. Your labs look stable. Blood sugar is a bit high, but your diabetes is manageable. Your heart function and kidney values are okay."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2183-03-31 06:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my foot? It’s still in the boot from last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2183-03-31 06:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The infection is healing. We’ll keep the silver sulfadiazine cream on it and you should follow up with podiatry."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2183-03-31 06:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my heart? I saw the ECG said fast rhythm."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2183-03-31 06:04:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you’re in atrial fibrillation with a slightly fast rate. But your metoprolol is helping. We’ll keep monitoring it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2183-03-31 06:05:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m scared this could happen again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2183-03-31 06:06:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand. But you’re taking the right steps now—staying on anticoagulation, managing blood pressure and diabetes. That reduces your risk significantly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2183-03-31 06:07:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the reading? It’s better, but still a little slow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2183-03-31 06:08:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That should continue to improve over the next few weeks. Some people benefit from speech or cognitive therapy if it doesn’t fully resolve."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2183-03-31 06:09:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do whatever helps."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2183-03-31 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning to discharge you today. Your neurologic exam is much improved. No deficits now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2183-03-31 10:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. What do I need to do when I get home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2183-03-31 10:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take your warfarin daily, check your INR in two days, and follow up with your PCP and the anticoagulation clinic. We’ve lowered your torsemide dose to help with fluid balance."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2183-03-31 10:03:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if I start having trouble reading again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2183-03-31 10:04:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Go to the ER immediately. Same for any weakness, speech issues, or dizziness. Also, weigh yourself daily—call if you gain more than three pounds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2183-03-31 10:05:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thank you, Doctor. I’ll stay on top of it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2183-03-31 10:06:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. You’ve made great progress. Take care, and we’ll see you in follow-up."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2183-03-30 02:22:00",
    "admission_end": "2183-03-31 14:00:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 50-year-old male with a history of atrial fibrillation, prior stroke, dilated cardiomyopathy, diabetes, and obesity was admitted for evaluation of acute difficulty reading words, with preserved speech and comprehension. Initial concern was for TIA, but neuroimaging revealed an acute ischemic infarct in the left medial temporal lobe, consistent with his symptoms. The stroke was attributed to cardioembolic etiology, likely due to a recent period of anticoagulation interruption when he self-discontinued Pradaxa due to stomach discomfort and had a gap before restarting warfarin. His symptoms improved during the hospital stay, and he was discharged neurologically stable. He remained on warfarin, with therapeutic INR at discharge, and was instructed to follow up with his anticoagulation clinic. Other management included continued treatment for heart failure, diabetes, and foot infection from a prior admission.",
    "problems": [
      "Cerebral embolism with cerebral infarction",
      "Other primary cardiomyopathies",
      "Congestive heart failure, unspecified",
      "Atrial flutter",
      "Atrial fibrillation",
      "Unspecified hereditary and idiopathic peripheral neuropathy",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Long-term (current) use of anticoagulants",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Obesity, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
